          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.512,
            0.55,
            0.531
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Sierpinski Triangle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.553,
            0.845,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "？ Show that by joining the midpoints of an equilateral triangle, we divide it into 4 identical equilateral triangles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.595,
            0.647,
            0.616
          ],
          "content": "[**Hint:** Note that the corner triangles are isosceles.]"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.618,
            0.669,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": "This fractal is called the **Sierpinski Triangle/Gasket**."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.647,
            0.294,
            0.67
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.673,
            0.845,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "1. Draw the initial few steps (at least till Step 2) of the shape sequence that leads to the Sierpinski Triangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.721,
            0.846,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "2. Find the number of holes, and the triangles that remain at each step of the shape sequence that leads to the Sierpinski Triangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.768,
            0.848,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": "3. Find the area of the region remaining at the nth step in each of the shape sequences that lead to the Sierpinski fractals. Take the area of the starting square/triangle to be 1 sq. unit."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
